{%- doc -%}
  Resolves one product spec value for the PDP Redo template.

  Metafield first, description parsing as a fallback, blank if neither yields
  anything (callers hide the row / line when it comes back blank).

  @param {object} product - the product object
  @param {string} field - one of:
      seat_height | total_height | width | depth   -> a bare number in cm ("65")
      fabric | frame | assembly                    -> a display string
      colour | design                              -> a display string, read
        straight off the description's "Colour:" / "Design:" line via
        snippets/pdp-redo-description-label.liquid (no metafield entry needed;
        colour ignores the metafields entirely, see below)

  Metafields read (all under the "custom" namespace, all optional):
      custom.seat_height    number / single line text
      custom.total_height   number / single line text
      custom.width          number / single line text
      custom.depth          number / single line text
      custom.fabric         single line text
      custom.frame          single line text
      custom.assembly       single line text
      custom.design         single line text

  COLOUR IS PARSED, NEVER READ FROM A METAFIELD
  ---------------------------------------------
  custom.color on this store is a Color metafield, so its .value is a ColorDrop
  and printing it renders the literal text "[ColorDrop]" instead of a colour
  name. The Colour row therefore always comes from the description's "Colour:"
  line, exactly like the Design row.

  DESCRIPTION FALLBACK
  --------------------
  Product descriptions on this store carry a details half in a fixed shape:

      Dimensions:
      W55cm x D50cm x H86cm  Seat Height 65cm
      Specifications:
      Material: Soft velvet upholstery, durable matte black metal frame
      Colour:   Cream (seat and backrest), Matte black (frame)
      Assembly: Simple Assembly Required

  We deliberately scan the WHOLE description rather than splitting on the "|"
  heading that the accordions use. That separator is written as <h3>|&nbsp;</h3>
  on at least some products (a non-breaking space), which the separator list in
  blocks/product-description-review.liquid and the accordion custom Liquid does
  not match — so splitting there silently yields nothing. The labels below only
  ever appear in the details half anyway, so scanning everything is safe.

  Parsing runs against a lower-cased copy because Liquid's split is
  case-sensitive and the labels are not consistently capitalised. Numeric fields
  are unaffected; the text fields are re-capitalised on the way out, so a parsed
  fallback reads "Simple assembly required" rather than the original casing.
  Populating the metafields replaces all of this with exact values.
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- liquid
  assign result = ''
  assign was_parsed = false

  comment
    1. Metafield wins whenever it is set.
  endcomment
  case field
    when 'seat_height'
      assign result = product.metafields.custom.seat_height.value
    when 'total_height'
      assign result = product.metafields.custom.total_height.value
    when 'width'
      assign result = product.metafields.custom.width.value
    when 'depth'
      assign result = product.metafields.custom.depth.value
    when 'fabric'
      assign result = product.metafields.custom.fabric.value
    when 'frame'
      assign result = product.metafields.custom.frame.value
    when 'assembly'
      assign result = product.metafields.custom.assembly.value
    when 'design'
      assign result = product.metafields.custom.design.value
  endcase

  assign result = result | default: '' | strip

  if result == blank
    comment
      2a. Colour and Design are read verbatim off their own line in the
      description's Specifications block, so they keep the copywriter's casing
      and punctuation. They are handled before the lower-cased scan below
      because that copy would destroy the casing.
    endcomment
    if field == 'colour'
      capture result
        render 'pdp-redo-description-label', product: product, labels: 'colour,color,colours,colors'
      endcapture
      assign result = result | strip
      assign was_parsed = true
    elsif field == 'design'
      capture result
        render 'pdp-redo-description-label', product: product, labels: 'design'
      endcapture
      assign result = result | strip
      assign was_parsed = true
    endif
  endif

  if result == blank
    comment
      2b. Description fallback for the remaining fields. One lower-cased,
      tag-free copy reused by every field so the description is only walked
      once.
    endcomment
    assign hay = product.description | strip_html | downcase
    assign was_parsed = true

    case field
      when 'seat_height'
        assign seat_parts = hay | split: 'seat height'
        if seat_parts.size > 1
          assign seat_raw = seat_parts[1] | split: 'cm' | first | replace: ':', '' | strip
          assign seat_num = seat_raw | times: 1
          if seat_num > 30 and seat_num < 130
            assign result = seat_raw
          endif
        endif

      when 'width' or 'depth' or 'total_height'
        comment
          The dimension line comes in two shapes across the catalogue:

              W55cm x D50cm x H86cm                     (Mia)
              Width 56cm x Depth 54cm x Height 97cm     (Camilla)

          So each chunk is classified by the spelled-out word first and only
          falls back to a single-letter prefix. Testing for the bare letter
          first is what broke this: "width" contains an "h", so the width chunk
          was read as the height and Camilla reported a 56cm total height under
          a 66cm seat.

          The segment is taken after the word "dimensions" (so its own letters
          cannot be classified) and cut at "seat height" (so the seat number is
          not read as the total height, and "Arm Rest Height 84cm", which sits
          after it on some products, is excluded too).
        endcomment
        assign dim_parts = hay | split: 'dimensions'
        if dim_parts.size > 1
          assign dim_segment = dim_parts[1]
          assign dim_segment = dim_segment | split: 'seat height' | first
          assign dim_segment = dim_segment | split: 'specification' | first

          assign wanted_axis = 'width'
          if field == 'depth'
            assign wanted_axis = 'depth'
          elsif field == 'total_height'
            assign wanted_axis = 'height'
          endif

          assign dim_chunks = dim_segment | split: ' x '
          for chunk in dim_chunks
            assign chunk_axis = ''

            if chunk contains 'width'
              assign chunk_axis = 'width'
            elsif chunk contains 'depth'
              assign chunk_axis = 'depth'
            elsif chunk contains 'height'
              assign chunk_axis = 'height'
            elsif chunk contains 'length'
              # Benches and tables label the long side 'Length'.
              assign chunk_axis = 'width'
            else
              assign compact = chunk | remove: ':' | strip
              if compact contains 'w'
                assign chunk_axis = 'width'
              elsif compact contains 'd'
                assign chunk_axis = 'depth'
              elsif compact contains 'h'
                assign chunk_axis = 'height'
              endif
            endif

            if result == blank and chunk_axis == wanted_axis
              comment
                Take the last whitespace-separated token before "cm", then peel
                off any axis prefix. Handles "width 56" -> 56 and "w55" -> 55.
              endcomment
              assign chunk_raw = chunk | split: 'cm' | first | split: ' ' | last
              assign chunk_raw = chunk_raw | remove: 'w' | remove: 'd' | remove: 'h' | remove: 'l' | remove: ':' | strip
              assign chunk_num = chunk_raw | times: 1
              if chunk_num > 10 and chunk_num < 400
                assign result = chunk_raw
              endif
            endif
          endfor
        endif

      when 'fabric'
        assign fabric_list = 'chenille:Chenille,boucle:Bouclé,velvet:Velvet,leather:Leather,faux fur:Faux fur,linen:Linen,cotton:Cotton,wool:Wool,fabric:Fabric'
        assign fabric_pairs = fabric_list | split: ','
        for pair in fabric_pairs
          if result == blank
            assign kv = pair | split: ':'
            assign needle = kv[0]
            if hay contains needle
              assign result = kv[1]
            endif
          endif
        endfor

      when 'frame'
        comment
          Composed from a finish word plus a frame material word, e.g.
          "matte black" + "metal" -> "Matte black metal". Both halves have to be
          found or the row stays hidden rather than guessing.
        endcomment
        assign finish_list = 'matte black:Matte black,brushed gold:Brushed gold,polished chrome:Polished chrome,black:Black,gold:Gold,chrome:Chrome,brass:Brass,silver:Silver,white:White,walnut:Walnut,oak:Oak'
        assign frame_material_list = 'powder-coated metal:powder-coated metal,stainless steel:stainless steel,metal:metal,steel:steel,wood:wood'

        assign frame_finish = ''
        assign finish_pairs = finish_list | split: ','
        for pair in finish_pairs
          if frame_finish == blank
            assign kv = pair | split: ':'
            assign needle = kv[0]
            if hay contains needle
              assign frame_finish = kv[1]
            endif
          endif
        endfor

        assign frame_material = ''
        assign frame_material_pairs = frame_material_list | split: ','
        for pair in frame_material_pairs
          if frame_material == blank
            assign kv = pair | split: ':'
            assign needle = kv[0]
            if hay contains needle
              assign frame_material = kv[1]
            endif
          endif
        endfor

        if frame_finish != blank and frame_material != blank
          assign result = frame_finish | append: ' ' | append: frame_material
        endif

      when 'assembly'
        comment
          Split on the label WITH its colon. Splitting on the bare word would
          also hit the word inside the value itself ("Assembly: Simple Assembly
          Required") and return just "Simple". newline_to_br then gives us a
          token to cut the line at, since Liquid cannot split on "\n".
        endcomment
        assign assembly_parts = hay | split: 'assembly:'
        if assembly_parts.size > 1
          assign assembly_raw = assembly_parts[1] | newline_to_br | split: '<br' | first | strip
          if assembly_raw != blank
            assign result = assembly_raw | capitalize
          endif
        endif
    endcase
  endif

  comment
    3. Every parsed text value is cut to a single clause so no row can wrap:
    keep whatever comes before the first comma or full stop, whichever lands
    first, and drop the punctuation with it. "Versatile design that complements
    a variety of interiors, from contemporary to transitional" becomes
    "Versatile design that complements a variety of interiors".

    This runs outside the fallback branch on purpose: colour and design resolve
    in step 2a, so a truncation nested inside step 2b would never see them.

    Only parsed values are cut. A metafield someone typed by hand is left
    exactly as entered, and numeric fields are excluded either way - a decimal
    like 45.5 would be cut at its own point and report 45 - so the CSS ellipsis
    in blocks/pdp-redo-specs.liquid is what keeps those on one line.
  endcomment
  assign single_line_fields = 'colour,design,fabric,frame,assembly' | split: ','

  if was_parsed and result != blank and single_line_fields contains field
    assign upto_comma = result | split: ',' | first
    assign upto_stop = result | split: '.' | first
    assign comma_size = upto_comma | size
    assign stop_size = upto_stop | size

    if stop_size < comma_size
      assign result = upto_stop
    else
      assign result = upto_comma
    endif

    assign result = result | strip

    comment
      A single clause can still outrun the column, so it is capped here as a
      backstop for a runaway sentence. The cap is deliberately generous: the
      CSS ellipsis is viewport-accurate and the real guarantee of one line, so
      a normal clause like "Versatile design that complements a variety of
      interiors" should reach the browser intact and be clipped only where it
      genuinely does not fit.
    endcomment
    assign result = result | truncate: 60, '…'
  endif
-%}
{{- result -}}
